# Project 1: myshell

## Course
COP4634 - Systems & Networks I  
Fall 2026

## Project Overview
This project implements a simplified Unix-style shell named `myshell`.

The shell reads a command entered by the user, parses the command into tokens, and stores the parsed information in a `Param` object. The parser recognizes normal command arguments, input redirection, output redirection, and background execution.

Part II of the project will extend the shell so that it creates child processes, executes programs, performs input/output redirection, supports foreground and background execution, and prevents zombie processes.

## Files
- `myshell.cpp` - contains `main()`, displays the shell prompt, reads user input, and passes commands to the parser.
- `parse.cpp` - implements command validation, tokenization, and conversion of tokens into a `Param` object.
- `parse.hpp` - declares the `Parse` class and parser functions.
- `param.cpp` - implements the `Param` class functions.
- `param.hpp` - defines the `Param` class and `MAXARGS`.
- `Makefile` - compiles and links the project.
- `README` - describes the project and its usage.

## Building the Program
From the project directory, run:

```bash
make
```

To remove the executable and object files, run:

```bash
make clean
```

The Makefile compiles the project with debugging information and compiler warnings enabled.

## Running the Program
Run the shell with:

```bash
./myshell
```

The shell displays the prompt:

```text
$$$
```

Enter `exit` to terminate the shell.

The required debug-mode syntax is:

```bash
./myshell -Debug
```

In debug mode, the parsed contents of the `Param` object should be printed after each command.

## Command Syntax
Commands consist of tokens separated by spaces, tabs, or newline characters.

The parser supports:

- normal command arguments
- input redirection using `<filename`
- output redirection using `>filename`
- background execution using `&`

Example:

```text
one two three <four >five &
```

This command is parsed as:

```text
InputRedirect: [four]
OutputRedirect: [five]
Background: [1]
ArgumentCount: [3]
ArgumentVector[0]: [one]
ArgumentVector[1]: [two]
ArgumentVector[2]: [three]
```

## Current Part I Functionality
The current implementation:

- displays the `$$$` prompt
- reads commands from standard input
- exits when the user enters `exit`
- tokenizes input using spaces, tabs, and newline characters
- stores normal arguments in `argumentVector`
- stores input and output redirection filenames
- detects the background operator `&`
- supports repeated command entry

## Part II
Part II will add:

- process creation using `fork()`
- program execution using an `exec()` family function
- input and output redirection
- foreground and background execution
- waiting for child processes
- prevention of zombie processes
- waiting for all remaining background children before the shell exits

## Notes
The project is intended to run on the department Linux servers.

Do not use the `system()` function or another production shell to execute commands.
